      passed=7 failed=2

  (b) as uid 1000, cwd /tmp/claude-0/f3-ubuntu, machine near-idle
      (su ubuntu, PATH=/usr/local/go/bin, GOMODCACHE/GOCACHE copied out of
      /root so the unprivileged user could build), ~40 min:
      identical matrix -- CHECK 02 and CHECK 03 FAIL, everything else PASS
      ---
      passed=7 failed=2
      ORACLE_EXIT=1

  WHY 02 AND 03 FAIL, established rather than assumed. Both nested oracles
  fail on exactly one sub-check each, and both are the same command:
    $ grep -n "package-suite-race" -B3 .swarm/tier3/F1/accept.sh
      168: go test -race -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/services/storage/
    $ grep -n "package-suite-race" -B3 .swarm/tier3/F2/accept.sh
      134: go test -race -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/services/storage/
    /tmp/f3_f1.out (uid-1000 run):  CHECK 02-package-suite-race: FAIL (want 0, got 1)
                                    everything else PASS, passed=6 failed=1
    /tmp/f3_f2.out (uid-1000 run):  CHECK 04-package-suite-race: FAIL (want 0, got 1)
                                    01,02,03,05,06 PASS, passed=5 failed=1
    $ grep -E "^(panic:|FAIL)" /tmp/f1_race.out /tmp/f2_race.out
      panic: test timed out after 10m0s
      FAIL  budget2/internal/services/storage  600.251s
      panic: test timed out after 10m0s
      FAIL  budget2/internal/services/storage  600.274s
  The stack at the timeout is scrypt inside age.Encrypt on an ordinary test,
  not a deadlock. Measured runtime of that same command on the idle box with
  a bigger budget (see criterion 4): 1026.965s, rc 0, "ok". So the F1/F2
  oracles' 600s budget is ~40% below what this 4-CPU host needs; on the host
  attempts 1-3 were graded on, it fit.
  This failure is NOT attributable to attempt 4: F1/F2 accept.sh are
  pre-existing artifacts, attempt 4 added no production code (criterion 3),
  and the suite they time out on is green given enough time (criterion 4).
  I am recording the criterion as unmet because it is unmet as written --
  the oracle exits 1 -- not because I found a defect in the work. What it
  would take to satisfy it here: raise the -timeout in F1's check 02 and F2's
  check 04 (a lead decision about the oracles, outside this task's scope), and
  run at uid != 0 (see criterion 4).

=====================================================================
CRITERION 2 -- checks 09 and 10 pass BECAUSE the two named tests detect
  their mutations.  MET for 09 and 10 at uid 1000; and I found that check 10
  is SPURIOUS at uid 0. Detail below, because this is the substantive finding.

  accept.sh's mutate_and_expect_failure only reports "suite detected the
  mutation", never which test, so I reproduced both mutations by hand,
  applying byte-for-byte the same python snippets accept.sh embeds
  (/tmp/claude-0/mut_enc.py, /tmp/claude-0/mut_aw.py) on fresh copies.

  Mutation 09 (drop `failed = append(failed, path)` from the encryptData
  error branch) -- confirmed to apply cleanly:
    $ cd /tmp/claude-0/f3-mut09 && git diff -- internal/services/storage/migration.go
      @@ -469,7 +469,6 @@ func (s *Storage) rollbackDecryptionWithRecipient(
              encrypted, err := encryptData(data, recipient)
              if err != nil {
      -                failed = append(failed, path)
                       s.invalidateCache(path)
    Named test, as uid 1000:
      --- FAIL: TestRollbackDecryptionReportsPathOnEncryptFailure (0.00s)
          migration_decrypt_rollback_test.go:362: expected failed=[.../plain.csv], got []
    Full package suite on the mutant as uid 0: the only two failures are that
    named test and TestRollbackDecryptionWithRecipientReportsUnrestorableFiles
    (the latter is the root artifact of criterion 4, red on the unmutated tree
    too). No other test in the package notices.

  Mutation 10 (drop `failed = append(failed, path)` from the atomicWrite
  error branch) -- applies cleanly on top of F4's filePerm change:
    $ cd /tmp/claude-0/f3-mut10 && git diff -- internal/services/storage/migration.go
      @@ -486,7 +486,6 @@
              } else if err := s.atomicWrite(path, encrypted, perm); err != nil {
      -                failed = append(failed, path)
                }
    FULL package suite on the mutant, as uid 1000 -- exactly one failure, the
    named test:
      $ storage-mut10.test (uid 1000, whole suite, -test.v)
        --- FAIL: TestRollbackDecryptionReportsPathOnAtomicWriteFailure (2.53s)
        FAIL
      (targeted run gives the assertion line:
        migration_decrypt_rollback_test.go:435: expected
        failed=[.../sub/plain.csv], got [])

  FINDING (check 10 is not evidence at uid 0). The new test opens with
  `if os.Geteuid() == 0 { t.Skip("root bypasses unix file permissions") }`
  (migration_decrypt_rollback_test.go:397-399), so as root it never runs:
    $ storage-mut10.test -test.run TestRollbackDecryptionReportsPathOnAtomicWriteFailure -test.v   # as root
        migration_decrypt_rollback_test.go:398: root bypasses unix file permissions
      --- SKIP: TestRollbackDecryptionReportsPathOnAtomicWriteFailure (0.00s)
      PASS
  The skip guard is correct engineering -- the mechanism genuinely cannot work
  as root -- but it means the oracle's "CHECK 10: PASS (suite detected the
  mutation)" in run (a) above was produced by the unrelated pre-existing root
  failure of criterion 4, not by the test the check is meant to grade. A root
  grader gets a green check 10 for the wrong reason. Only the uid-1000
  reproduction above actually establishes check 10.

  Both tests assert what their names claim (read in full,
  migration_decrypt_rollback_test.go:328-450): 09 writes a plaintext file,
  calls s.rollbackDecryptionWithRecipient([]string{plainFile},
  failingRecipient{}) and requires failed == [full path] plus byte-identical
  on-disk content; 10 chmods the *containing directory* to 0500 so
  os.CreateTemp for atomicWrite's staging file fails, and requires the same.
  Neither is a name-only test.

